<h2>Elf</h2>

<p>
The Elf XML directory includes more generic Elf configurations. These are not specific to any particular Elf computer.
</p>

<p><strong>chip8.xml</strong></p>
<p>
Chip-8 configuration for the Elf. By default, <strong>Blinky</strong> is selected as the Chip-8 game.
</p>
<p>
The Chip-8 interpreter is loaded from 0000-01FF (hexadecimal) and 0E00-0E6D. The selected Chip-8 software loads from 0200.
</p>
<p>
Use the <strong>LOAD</strong> button to load different Chip-8 games at 0200 (hexadecimal).
</p>

<p><strong>elfos-biosio-&lt;type&gt;.xml</strong></p>
<p>
These XML configurations are for running Elf/Os on the indicated video type with BIOSIO.
</p>

<p><strong>elfos-installation-&lt;type&gt;.xml</strong></p>
<p>
These XML configurations are for installation of Elf/Os on the indicated video type with or without BIOSIO.
</p>

<p><strong>elfos-serial.xml</strong></p>
<p>
This XML configuration is for running Elf/Os on Serial I/O.
</p>

<p><strong>figforth-uart.xml</strong></p>
<p>
This XML configuration loads and runs Fig Forth for the CDP1802.
</p>
<p>
This version of Forth uses a terminal connected to a CDP1854 UART, for which basic support is implemented in the emulator.
</p>

<p><strong>game-elfinvad.xml</strong></p>
<p>
This XML configuration runs most games. By default, <strong>Invaders</strong> is selected, but this can be changed to any of the other provided game files.
</p>

<p><strong>musicmachine.xml</strong></p>
<p>
Basic XML configuration to run the <strong>Music Maker</strong> and Christmas songs as published in <strong>Ipso Facto</strong> (Issue 5 and 8).
</p>
<p>
By default, <code>Music Maker.hex</code> and the song <code>O Christmas Tree.hex</code> are loaded.
</p>
<p>
Some songs require a different speed, which is indicated in the song file name (Double, 1_2, etc.). To play these, also load the equivalent <strong>Music Maker</strong> file.
</p>
<p>
The <strong>Ipso Facto</strong> articles can be found on <a href="http://www.cosmacelf.com">COSMAC ELF</a> and are also included in the Elf/Music directory.
</p>

<p><strong>rcabasic3-&lt;type&gt;.xml</strong></p>
<p>
These XML configurations load and run RCA Basic.
</p>

<p><strong>rcabug-serial.xml</strong></p>
<p>
RCA Monitor program as described in <strong>IPSO Facto</strong> number 10.
</p>

<p><strong>rommapper-&lt;type&gt;.xml</strong></p>
<p>
<strong>Richard's ROM Mapper</strong> uses <code>OUT 7</code> to select a 32K slot from a large ROM chip.
</p>
<p>
The selected ROM is switched to 8000-FFFF (hexadecimal) using A15 from bit 0 on port 7, A16 from bit 1, etc.
</p>
<p>
After starting the Elf, press <code>RETURN</code> and type <code>RUN</code>.
</p>
<p>
To load a program, type the program number or <code>N</code> for the next page.
</p>

<p><strong>superbasic-tapeloader.xml</strong></p>
<p>
Quest <strong>Super Basic</strong> loader program.
</p>

<p><strong>superbasic&lt;version&gt;-&lt;type&gt;.xml</strong></p>
<p>
These XML configurations load and run Quest <strong>Super Basic</strong> (version is indicated in the filename).
</p>

<p><strong>superbasic5.0+minimon-serial.xml</strong></p>
<p>
EPROM board XML configuration including a Mini Monitor and Quest <strong>Super BASIC REL 5.0</strong>.
</p>
<p>
This ROM file was created by Eric Tchong. <strong>Super BASIC</strong> itself was designed by Ron Cenker.
</p>
<p>
<strong>Super BASIC</strong> can be started from the monitor by pressing <code>B</code>.
</p>
<p>
For documentation on these ROMs, see the file section of the CosmacElf Yahoo group.
</p>

<p><strong>supergoldmonitor-serial.xml</strong></p>
<p>
EPROM board XML configuration including the <strong>Super Gold Monitor</strong> and assembler.
</p>
<p>
This ROM file was created by Eric Tchong.
</p>
<p>
For documentation about this ROM, see the file section of the CosmacElf Yahoo group.
</p>

<p><strong>tinybasic-interpreter.xml</strong></p>
<p>
This XML configuration loads and runs the <strong>Tiny Basic Interpreter</strong> for the COSMAC CDP1802, version 2.
</p>
<p>
Designed by Robert Edwards, including output routines for an MC6847 video chip.
</p>

<p><strong>ut4-serial.xml</strong></p>
<p>
Basic UT4 XML configuration with 62 KB RAM.
</p>
<p>
UT4 runs in ROM at 8000-81FF (hexadecimal).
</p>

<p><strong>vdu-ascii-tinybasic.xml</strong></p>
<p>
VDU / MC6847 XML configuration with ASCII keyboard.
</p>

<p><strong>vdu-hex-game.xml</strong></p>
<p>
VDU / MC6847 configuration with a hex keypad.
</p>


<h2>FRED I</h2>

<p><strong>21 I.xml</strong></p>
<p>
FRED I configuration running <strong>21 I</strong>.
</p>

<p>
Use punch cards as follows:
</p>
<pre>
0   BD   New game / player one card
1   CC   Give player one card
2   78   Give dealer one card
3   73   Show hand player
4   B1   Show hand dealer
</pre>

<p><strong>add drill.xml</strong></p>
<p>
FRED I configuration running <strong>Add Drill</strong>.
</p>

<p>
This software requires spoken text and tones recorded on the original tape and included in <code>Add Drill.wav</code> (if not available, it will be downloaded).
</p>
<p>
The start card is requested with card <code>0</code>, FE (hexadecimal).
</p>
<p>
Press the start card two times and, after the tone, a third time.
</p>
<p>
Then type the result of the addition question when it appears.
</p>

<p><strong>animate demo.xml</strong></p>
<p>
FRED I configuration running the <strong>Animate Demo</strong>.
</p>
<p>
Demo that loads data from tape and displays it on the screen.
</p>
<p>
The data is included in <code>Animate Demo Data.wav</code> (if not available, it will be downloaded).
</p>

<p>
The same data is also included in binary form in <code>Animate demo data.bin</code>, but this file cannot be loaded directly in Emma 02.
</p>

<p><strong>bowling.xml</strong></p>
<p>
FRED I configuration running <strong>Bowling</strong>.
</p>

<p>
Two players: <code>X</code> and <code>O</code>. Five frames, two balls per frame.
</p>

<p><strong>Controls</strong></p>
<pre>
Any key           Roll ball
F1, F2, etc.      Frame number indication
X1, X2            Player X, ball number
O1, O2            Player O, ball number
RESET/RUN         New game
</pre>

<p>
This game uses sounds on tape when a ball is rolled. The sounds are recordings of a bowling ball hitting targets, repeated multiple times.
</p>

<p>
The file used here is <code>Bowling.wav</code>, which includes the rolling ball once (if not available, it will be downloaded).
</p>

<p>
This file (with only one rolling ball sound) can be used in Emma 02, as Emma 02 restarts the tape after every play, resulting in better synchronization compared to using a file with repeated sounds.
</p>

<p><strong>cardtran.xml</strong></p>
<p>
FRED I configuration running the <strong>CARDTRAN Interpreter</strong>.
</p>

<p>
See <strong>Cardtran.readme</strong> or <strong>Cardtran.asm</strong> for more information.
</p>

<p><strong>clue.xml</strong></p>
<p>
FRED I configuration running <strong>Clue</strong>.
</p>

<p>
As described in the <a href="https://www.retrotechnology.com/memship/Sarnoff_FRED_manual_W_R_H.pdf">manual</a>.
</p>

<p>
This game uses spoken text and tones recorded on the original tape and included in <code>Clue.wav</code> (if not available, it will be downloaded).
</p>

<p><strong>deduce-leds.xml</strong></p>
<p>
FRED I configuration running <strong>Deduce-leds</strong>.
</p>

<p>
As described in the <a href="https://www.retrotechnology.com/memship/Sarnoff_FRED_manual_W_R_H.pdf">manual</a>.
</p>

<p>
This game uses spoken text and tones recorded on the original tape and included in <code>Deduce-leds.wav</code> (if not available, it will be downloaded).
</p>

<p><strong>demo.xml</strong></p>
<p>
FRED I configuration running a demo using spoken text and tones recorded on the original tape and included in <code>Demo.wav</code> (if not available, it will be downloaded).
</p>

<p><strong>flip I.xml</strong></p>
<p>
FRED I configuration running <strong>Flip I</strong>.
</p>
<p>
Start the game with card <code>0</code> (twice).
</p>
<p>
The game uses two players: white and black square. The first player to get eight squares of his type wins. The centre square indicates which player gets the next turn.
</p>
<p>
Use card <code>1</code> or 6D (hexadecimal), <code>2</code> or 97 (hexadecimal) or <code>3</code> or 9E (hexadecimal) to flip squares.
</p>
<p>
If no player wins by turn 50, the player with the most squares wins.
</p>

<p><strong>Match I.xml</strong></p>
<p>
FRED I configuration running <strong>Match I</strong>.
</p>
<p>
This game is for two players (<code>1</code> and <code>2</code>). The object is to find matching pairs of symbols.
</p>
<p>
Press card <code>0</code> or FF (hexadecimal) in BYTE mode to start the game.
</p>
<p>
A 4 x 4 array of symbols is shown on the screen. The 16 keyboard positions represent symbol positions.
</p>
<p>
Player 1 selects two positions to find matching symbols. If a match is found, they are replaced by <code>1</code> on the screen and the player gets another turn.
</p>
<p>
If no match is found, it is player 2's turn. Turns alternate until all symbols are matched.
</p>
<p>
Count the number of <code>1</code> and <code>2</code> values to determine the winner. New game = <code>RESET</code>/<code>RUN</code>.
</p>

<p><strong>memory test.xml</strong></p>
<p>
FRED I configuration running <strong>Memory Test</strong>.
</p>
<p>
Press a value between <code>00</code> and <code>80</code>, followed by a second byte.
</p>
<p>
The second byte is stored in memory at 01xx (hexadecimal), where xx is the first byte.
</p>
<p>
A binary representation is shown on the screen. The screen has 128 positions for the 128 memory locations.
</p>

<p><strong>nimnet.xml</strong></p>
<p>
FRED I configuration running <strong>Nimnet</strong>.
</p>
<p>
This game uses cards with codes A1/A2, B1-B4, C1-C5, D1-D5, E1-E4 and F1/F2 (hexadecimal).
</p>
<p>
The objective is to create a path from the left dot to the right dot using random cards.
</p>

<p><strong>prog apt test.xml</strong></p>
<p>
FRED I configuration running <strong>Prog. Apt. Test</strong>.
</p>
<p>
This software requires spoken text and tones recorded on the original tapes and included in <code>Prog. Apt. Test.wav</code> (if not available, it will be downloaded).
</p>

<p><strong>slide puzzle I.xml</strong></p>
<p>
FRED I configuration running <strong>Slide Puzzle I</strong>.
</p>
<p>
Start the game with card <code>0</code>, followed by card <code>1</code> or 31 (hexadecimal), <code>2</code> or 33 (hexadecimal), <code>3</code> or 35 (hexadecimal).
</p>
<p>
Card <code>1</code>, <code>2</code> or <code>3</code> selects the game type.
</p>

<p><strong>Controls</strong></p>
<pre>
6    Up
12   Left
13   Right (FF)
18   Down (FA)
</pre>

<p>
For card <code>2</code> and <code>3</code>, create a pattern symmetrical about the centre row in a minimum number of turns (10 for card <code>2</code>).
</p>
<p>
For card <code>1</code>, swap the full and empty squares while keeping the rest unchanged within 18 turns.
</p>


